    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d287">

                <pb facs="0510" n="408" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg408"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.114 Great Britain/437: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Atherton</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-01-23T16:00:00+00:00">January 23, 1935—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 23—1:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>31. I stressed to Vansittart<anchor n="25" xml:id="d287-f1"/> today the substance
                    of your 12, January 22, 6 p.m., and he informed me Lindsay had ably set forth in
                    a telegram just received the emphasis Mr. Phillips had put on an apparent lack
                    of British cooperation. Vansittart explained that a meeting of the governmental
                    departments concerned had been urgently summoned and he would send for me at an
                    early date to <pb facs="0511" n="409" xml:id="pg409"/> advise me further as to
                    the course of these deliberations. In the meantime he asked that if any measures
                    of the French or Canadian authorities had in the Department’s knowledge proven
                    outstandingly effective, these measures might be outlined to Lindsay in order
                    that the British might have the benefit thereof in their deliberations.<anchor
                        n="26" xml:id="d287-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Atherton</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d287-f1" n="25" xml:id="d287-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Robert Gilbert Vansittart, British Permanent Under Secretary of State for
                    Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d287-f2" n="26" xml:id="d287-f2-content">
                <p>An undated marginal notation reads as follows: “Sec. Morgenthau and his
                    assistants were talking to Osborne about this and have already furnished
                    information about cooperation we are receiving from other govt’s. J[ohn] D.
                    H[ickerson].”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
